#: ../../source/tough-cookie_README.md:44
msgid "`parseDate(string)`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tough-cookie_README.md:46
msgid ""
"Parse a cookie date string into a `Date`.  Parses according to RFC6265 "
"Section 5.1.1, not `Date.parse()`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tough-cookie_README.md:48
msgid "`formatDate(date)`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tough-cookie_README.md:50
msgid "Format a Date into a RFC1123 string (the RFC6265-recommended format)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tough-cookie_README.md:52
msgid "`canonicalDomain(str)`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tough-cookie_README.md:54
msgid ""
"Transforms a domain-name into a canonical domain-name.  The canonical "
"domain-name is a trimmed, lowercased, stripped-of-leading-dot and "
"optionally punycode-encoded domain-name (Section 5.1.2 of RFC6265).  For "
"the most part, this function is idempotent (can be run again on its "
"output without ill effects)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tough-cookie_README.md:56
msgid "`domainMatch(str,domStr[,canonicalize=true])`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tough-cookie_README.md:58
msgid ""
"Answers \"does this real domain match the domain in a cookie?\".  The "
"`str` is the \"current\" domain-name and the `domStr` is the \"cookie\" "
"domain-name.  Matches according to RFC6265 Section 5.1.3, but it helps to"
" think of it as a \"suffix match\"."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tough-cookie_README.md:60
msgid ""
"The `canonicalize` parameter will run the other two parameters through "
"`canonicalDomain` or not."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tough-cookie_README.md:62
msgid "`defaultPath(path)`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tough-cookie_README.md:64
msgid ""
"Given a current request/response path, gives the Path apropriate for "
"storing in a cookie.  This is basically the \"directory\" of a \"file\" "
"in the path, but is specified by Section 5.1.4 of the RFC."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tough-cookie_README.md:66
msgid ""
"The `path` parameter MUST be _only_ the pathname part of a URI (i.e. "
"excludes the hostname, query, fragment, etc.).  This is the `.pathname` "
"property of node's `uri.parse()` output."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tough-cookie_README.md:68
msgid "`pathMatch(reqPath,cookiePath)`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tough-cookie_README.md:70
msgid ""
"Answers \"does the request-path path-match a given cookie-path?\" as per "
"RFC6265 Section 5.1.4.  Returns a boolean."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tough-cookie_README.md:72
msgid ""
"This is essentially a prefix-match where `cookiePath` is a prefix of "
"`reqPath`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tough-cookie_README.md:74
msgid "`parse(cookieString[, options])`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tough-cookie_README.md:76
msgid "alias for `Cookie.parse(cookieString[, options])`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tough-cookie_README.md:78
msgid "`fromJSON(string)`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tough-cookie_README.md:80
msgid "alias for `Cookie.fromJSON(string)`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tough-cookie_README.md:82
msgid "`getPublicSuffix(hostname)`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tough-cookie_README.md:84
msgid ""
"Returns the public suffix of this hostname.  The public suffix is the "
"shortest domain-name upon which a cookie can be set.  Returns `null` if "
"the hostname cannot have cookies set for it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tough-cookie_README.md:86
msgid ""
"For example: `www.example.com` and `www.subdomain.example.com` both have "
"public suffix `example.com`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tough-cookie_README.md:88
msgid ""
"For further information, see http://publicsuffix.org/.  This module "
"derives its list from that site. This call is currently a wrapper around "
"[`psl`](https://www.npmjs.com/package/psl)'s [get() "
"method](https://www.npmjs.com/package/psl#pslgetdomain)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tough-cookie_README.md:90
msgid "`cookieCompare(a,b)`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tough-cookie_README.md:92
msgid ""
"For use with `.sort()`, sorts a list of cookies into the recommended "
"order given in the RFC (Section 5.4 step 2). The sort algorithm is, in "
"order of precedence:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tough-cookie_README.md:94
msgid "Longest `.path`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tough-cookie_README.md:95
msgid "oldest `.creation` (which has a 1ms precision, same as `Date`)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tough-cookie_README.md:96
msgid "lowest `.creationIndex` (to get beyond the 1ms precision)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tough-cookie_README.md:103
msgid ""
"**Note**: Since JavaScript's `Date` is limited to a 1ms precision, "
"cookies within the same milisecond are entirely possible. This is "
"especially true when using the `now` option to `.setCookie()`. The "
"`.creationIndex` property is a per-process global counter, assigned "
"during construction with `new Cookie()`. This preserves the spirit of the"
" RFC sorting: older cookies go first. This works great for "
"`MemoryCookieStore`, since `Set-Cookie` headers are parsed in order, but "
"may not be so great for distributed systems. Sophisticated `Store`s may "
"wish to set this to some other _logical clock_ such that if cookies A and"
" B are created in the same millisecond, but cookie A is created before "
"cookie B, then `A.creationIndex < B.creationIndex`. If you want to alter "
"the global counter, which you probably _shouldn't_ do, it's stored in "
"`Cookie.cookiesCreated`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tough-cookie_README.md:105
msgid "`permuteDomain(domain)`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tough-cookie_README.md:107
msgid ""
"Generates a list of all possible domains that `domainMatch()` the "
"parameter.  May be handy for implementing cookie stores."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tough-cookie_README.md:109
msgid "`permutePath(path)`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/tough-cookie_README.md:111
msgid ""
"Generates a list of all possible paths that `pathMatch()` the parameter."
"  May be handy for implementing cookie stores."
msgstr ""

